# vendor_init needs write access to the vendor_internal_prop types this device
# declares in property.te. Without these, every one of those properties is
# silently dropped the moment SELinux goes enforcing.
#
# Why it is needed at all: property.te turns a set of prefixes that used to fall
# through to vendor_default_prop into dedicated vendor_internal_prop types. That
# is the right call for privilege separation, but vendor_init's default write
# access covers vendor_default_prop ONLY, so labelling a property that
# /vendor/build.prop assigns takes away the permission that made it work.
#
# ⚠️ property.te:8-13 justifies the labelling by claiming vendor_default_prop
# cannot be set by vendor_init. That is INVERTED. The neverallow at
# system/sepolicy/private/domain.te:841 explicitly excludes vendor_init, and
# private/vendor_init.te:312 grants it. Do not reason from that comment.
#
# Measured, not assumed. Both of these appear in work/first-boot/s12-boot1:
#
#   avc: denied { set } property=vendor.thermal.mode
#        scontext=u:r:vendor_init:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:vendor_thermal_prop:s0
#   avc: denied { set } property=persist.vendor.lgsi.adbdebug
#        scontext=u:r:vendor_init:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:vendor_lenovotb_init_prop:s0
#
# They do not break anything today only because this is a permissive bring-up
# build -- CheckMacPerms (system/core/init/property_service.cpp:163-177) returns
# success when permissive, so the property is stored anyway.
#
# What each one costs under enforcing:
#
#   vendor_thermal_prop        vendor.thermal.mode is what gates the whole
#                              on property:vendor.thermal.mode=* block in
#                              init.qcom.rc:524. Lose it and thermal-switch-engine
#                              never starts and none of the seven
#                              thermal-engine-malbec-*.conf policies load. That is
#                              exactly the failure session 11 added the property to
#                              fix, so losing it silently undoes that work.
#
#   vendor_lenovotb_init_prop  init.target.rc:270 does
#                              `setprop persist.vendor.lgsi.adbdebug
#                               ${ro.boot.lgsi.adbdebug}` from a vendor rc, and the
#                              same denial covers vendor_init READING the
#                              ${ro.odm.*} values it expands elsewhere in that file.
#
# Pattern and precedent: device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr/sm8750/generic/vendor/common/
# vendor_init.te:75-109 does exactly this for QTI's own vendor_internal_prop types.
set_prop(vendor_init, vendor_thermal_prop)
set_prop(vendor_init, vendor_lenovotb_init_prop)

# ⚠️ NOT granted, deliberately: vendor_init -> build_prop / build_bootimage_prop.
#
# Two more property-set denials show up on every boot:
#
#   avc: denied { set } property=ro.system.build.fingerprint     tcontext=build_prop
#   avc: denied { set } property=ro.bootimage.build.fingerprint  tcontext=build_bootimage_prop
#
# The source is the factory init.target.rc:278-283, `on property:
# ro.odm.fingerprint.update=*`, which rewrites the fingerprint across vendor /
# system / product / odm / bootimage. That is Lenovo's per-unit fingerprint
# mechanism and it must NOT be granted here:
#
#   1. It cannot succeed anyway. Those are ro.* properties, which are write-once,
#      and build.prop has already set them by the time this trigger fires.
#   2. If it ever did succeed it would overwrite the fingerprint PixelOS manages
#      (SetSafetyNetProps(), system/core/init/property_service.cpp:1377-1389).
#
# The denial is expected noise, not a defect. Leave it.
